// File: t4-azure-permission-bridges-isolate.kql   [STAGING]
//
// Domain: AZURE CONTROL PLANE (Entra audit + Azure Activity). NOT endpoint —
// this family reads AuditLogs / AzureActivity, keyed on a principal object-id,
// with its own shared functions (azure-control-plane/shared-functions.kql).
//
// Tactic: PRIVILEGE ESCALATION / PERSISTENCE across Azure's disjoint permission
// planes (TA0004 / TA0003). Models the Sysdig TRT "Azure permission takeover"
// kill chain — see ../references/2026-07-14-sysdig-azure-permission-takeover.md.
//
// TIER: T4 (isolate). These are the "alert on every occurrence" cross-plane
// BRIDGES the report calls high-signal / low-noise — the exact moves an attacker
// uses to hop between permission systems, each near-zero legitimate use.
//
// HEADER: MINIMAL (excludeAdmin = false) on every block. The whole point is
// elevated NHI activity; excluding privileged/automation principals would blind
// the rule to the very thing it hunts. Vetted automation goes on the SHORT
// KnownAutomationPrincipals allow-list in shared-functions.kql and is only
// honored where a block explicitly passes true.
//
// OPERATOR NOTE: multi-word / path op strings use `in~` (exact), `startswith`,
// or `contains` (substring) — NOT `has`/`has_any`, which are term-indexed and
// do not match phrases or `a/b/c` paths reliably. AzureActivity op strings are
// lowercased first (`_op`) so a single casing convention is matched.
//
// FOUR BLOCKS, each its OWN analytics rule:
//   Block 1 (static, AuditLogs)   — Entra-plane bridges: elevateAccess + an SP
//                                    self-granting a privileged directory role.
//   Block 2 (static, AzureActivity) — bearer-key mints (listKeys) + Key Vault
//                                    access-policy self-grant (resource-local).
//   Block 3 (behavior, union)     — THE CROSS-PLANE CHAIN: one principal touches
//                                    >=2 planes incl. a bridge/key-harvest step
//                                    inside a window. The piece native consoles
//                                    cannot express, and the reason this file
//                                    exists.
//
// COVERAGE CAVEAT (from the incident): elevateAccess lives in the Entra audit
// log, NOT in AzureActivity, despite being a resource-plane grant. Its exact
// OperationName can vary by tenant routing — CONFIRM the Block 1 elevateAccess
// term against your own telemetry before go-live (same discipline as the
// endpoint suite's "confirm wevtutil qe matches your term form"). Block 3 catches
// it via the union regardless of which table it lands in.


// ============================================================
// Rule: T4-Azure-entra-bridge-isolate   (static — AuditLogs / Entra plane)
// ============================================================
FilteredAuditLogs(false)   // MINIMAL — keep every principal; see shared-functions.kql
| where Result =~ "success"
| extend _tr = tostring(TargetResources)
| where
    // elevateAccess: GA -> User Access Administrator at root (/). The single call
    // that crosses "owns the directory" -> "owns every resource" (TA0004). Near-
    // zero benign use. CONFIRM this term vs. your telemetry (see header caveat).
    (OperationName contains "elevateAccess" or OperationName contains "Elevate access")
    // A SERVICE PRINCIPAL grants a privileged directory role (incl. to itself) —
    // the RoleManagement.ReadWrite.Directory tenant-takeover capability in action
    // (T1098.003). Actor is an app; target role is tier-0. ("Company Administrator"
    // is GA's legacy directoryRole name and still appears in some tenants.)
    or (ActorIsServicePrincipal
        and OperationName startswith "Add member to role"
        and _tr matches regex @"(Global|Company|Privileged Role|User Access|Privileged Authentication) Administrator")
| extend Severity = "Critical"
| project TimeGenerated, OperationName, Principal, PrincipalName, ActorIsServicePrincipal,
    TargetResources, Result, CorrelationId
| order by TimeGenerated desc


// ============================================================
// Rule: T4-Azure-resource-bridge-isolate   (static — AzureActivity / RBAC plane)
// Bearer-key MINTS + the Key Vault access-policy self-grant. The listKeys call is
// your ONLY chance to see a bearer key: it converts an identity-scoped grant into
// an identity-less secret whose every later use is invisible (Plane 4). Miss this
// event and the access it created is permanent and untraceable.
// ============================================================
FilteredAzureActivity(false)   // MINIMAL — keep every caller
| extend _op = tolower(OperationNameValue)
| where
    // storageAccounts/listKeys — storage account shared-key harvest (T1552.001 /
    // credential access -> identity-less data-plane access).
    _op contains "microsoft.storage/storageaccounts/listkeys"
    // Event Hub namespace listKeys — root SAS for the telemetry transport itself.
    // In the incident this stole the keys to the tenant's own audit pipeline; the
    // report flags it as the one to alert on LOUDEST (attacker goes after your
    // visibility next). (T1552.001 / TA0005 defense evasion of the log pipeline.)
    or _op contains "microsoft.eventhub/namespaces/authorizationrules/listkeys"
    // Function App host keys — bearer access to invoke/tamper functions (T1552.001).
    or _op contains "microsoft.web/sites/host/listkeys"
    or _op contains "/functions/listkeys"
    // Service Bus listKeys — same identity-less-key class.
    or _op contains "microsoft.servicebus/namespaces/authorizationrules/listkeys"
    // Key Vault access-policy SELF-GRANT (resource-local Plane 3). Adds the caller
    // to the vault's OWN access-policy list — a permission store the RBAC blade
    // never shows. Paired in the incident with roleAssignments/write on the same
    // vault to cover both auth models at once (T1098 / T1555).
    or _op contains "microsoft.keyvault/vaults/accesspolicies/write"
| extend Severity = "Critical"
| project TimeGenerated, OperationNameValue, Principal, CallerIpAddress,
    ResourceGroup, _ResourceId, ActivityStatusValue
| order by TimeGenerated desc


// ============================================================
// Rule: T4-Azure-cross-plane-escalation-chain-isolate   (behavior — the union)
// THE cross-plane correlation the native consoles can't give you. Normalizes the
// Entra plane (AuditLogs) and the resource plane (AzureActivity) onto ONE
// `Principal` timeline, then fires when a SINGLE principal crosses >=2 planes and
// touches a bridge or key-harvest step inside the window. Turns
//   "SP self-grants GA -> elevates to root UAA -> assigns Owner -> harvests keys"
// into ONE alertable chain instead of five unrelated log lines in two tables.
//
// This is the same normalize-then-summarize-by-key shape as the endpoint
// collect->egress bridge, but joined on IDENTITY (Principal) instead of DeviceId,
// because that is the only key these planes share once normalized.
//
// TUNING: window and thresholds are staging starts. If legitimate break-glass /
// tenant-recovery flows (a human GA who genuinely runs elevateAccess then fixes
// RBAC) trip it, exclude that identity via KnownAutomationPrincipals — do NOT
// widen the step list. Sentinel scheduled-rule shape: the summarize drops any
// per-row id, so map the entity on `Principal` for incident grouping.
// ============================================================
let lookback = 1d;
let window = 2h;
let escalation =
    union
      ( FilteredAuditLogs(false)
        | where Result =~ "success"
        | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
        | extend Plane = "Entra", Action = tolower(OperationName) ),
      ( FilteredAzureActivity(false)
        | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
        | extend Plane = "RBAC", Action = tolower(OperationNameValue) )
    // keep only the escalation-relevant verbs so the timeline is signal, not chatter
    | where Action matches regex "elevateaccess|add member to role|roleassignments/write|storageaccounts/listkeys|authorizationrules/listkeys|host/listkeys|vaults/accesspolicies/write"
    // tag each row so the aggregation can reason about step KIND, not array substrings
    | extend IsElevate      = Action contains "elevateaccess"
    | extend IsHarvest      = (Action contains "storageaccounts/listkeys"
                                or Action contains "authorizationrules/listkeys"
                                or Action contains "host/listkeys")
    | extend IsAccessPolicy = Action contains "vaults/accesspolicies/write";
escalation
| summarize
    Planes       = make_set(Plane),
    Steps        = make_set(Action),
    StepCount    = dcount(Action),
    ElevateCount = countif(IsElevate),
    BridgeCount  = countif(IsElevate or IsHarvest or IsAccessPolicy),
    FirstStep    = min(TimeGenerated),
    LastStep     = max(TimeGenerated)
    by Principal, TimeBin = bin(TimeGenerated, window)
// cross-plane, AND either the marquee bridge (elevateAccess) fired, or the
// principal ran a broad chain (3+ distinct verbs) that includes a bridge/harvest.
| where array_length(Planes) >= 2
    and (ElevateCount > 0 or (StepCount >= 3 and BridgeCount > 0))
| extend Severity = "Critical"
| project FirstStep, LastStep, Principal, Planes, Steps, StepCount, ElevateCount, BridgeCount, TimeBin
| order by FirstStep asc


// ============================================================
// NOTES / BACKLOG
// - roleAssignments/write (subscription-scope Owner self-assign) is intentionally
//   NOT a standalone isolate line: on its own it is too common in normal IaC. It
//   earns its severity only IN the Block 3 chain (as one of the 3+ verbs) or in a
//   dedicated T3 respond rule scoped to root ("/") scope. Add that T3 if the
//   chain proves too tight.
// - Key Vault DATA-plane reads (SecretGet/KeyGet) after an access-policy self-
//   grant would be the confirming follow-through, but AzureDiagnostics data-plane
//   logging is OFF by default — wire FilteredKeyVaultDiag into a Block-3 variant
//   only once you've confirmed those logs actually flow (see shared-functions.kql).
// - MicrosoftGraphActivityLogs (Graph-plane recon, the app-permission grant that
//   started the whole incident) is covered in the sibling T3 NHI-persistence file.
